WEBVTT

00:04.650 --> 00:07.725
Overhead power lines kill people at mines and construction

00:07.725 --> 00:10.862
sites every year, and the person killed is often

00:10.862 --> 00:13.562
not the operator. It's the person standing next

00:13.562 --> 00:15.950
to the truck, or holding a tag line,

00:15.950 --> 00:17.863
or walking up to see what happened.

00:17.863 --> 00:19.700
You don't have to touch a high-voltage line to

00:19.700 --> 00:22.875
be hurt. Electricity can arc across the gap to

00:22.875 --> 00:24.975
equipment that gets too close.

00:26.100 --> 00:28.738
On our sites, the equipment that reaches up is

00:28.738 --> 00:31.663
the danger: a haul truck or a tri-axle with its

00:31.663 --> 00:35.325
bed raised, a loader bucket lifted to load or dump,

00:35.325 --> 00:38.000
a drill mast being raised or moved,

00:38.000 --> 00:40.538
a crane or boom truck in the shop yard.

00:40.538 --> 00:43.113
Tri-axle road trucks haul inside the pit at all

00:43.113 --> 00:45.988
of our sites, so a raised bed near a line is a

00:45.988 --> 00:49.188
real risk, not a rare one. Someone standing on

00:49.188 --> 00:51.700
a load to tarp it is also reaching up,

00:51.700 --> 00:52.725
with his body.

00:56.038 --> 01:02.663
The MSHA standard, 30 CFR 56 and 57.12071,

01:02.663 --> 01:05.775
covers moving or operating equipment near energized

01:05.775 --> 01:08.638
high-voltage power lines. When the clearance is

01:08.638 --> 01:12.075
less than 10 feet, the lines have to be deenergized

01:12.075 --> 01:14.863
or other precautionary measures have to be taken.

01:14.863 --> 01:17.563
Ten feet is the line the standard draws.

01:17.563 --> 01:20.050
It is not a target to work up to. And it's harder

01:20.050 --> 01:22.425
to judge than it looks: from a cab,

01:22.425 --> 01:26.025
distances overhead are almost impossible to estimate,

01:26.025 --> 01:29.363
and lines sag and sway on a hot or windy day.

01:30.496 --> 01:33.534
Our rule is simpler and stricter. No dumping and

01:33.534 --> 01:36.409
no parking near overhead lines. No raised beds

01:36.409 --> 01:38.621
near them. And no tarping near them.

01:38.621 --> 01:40.683
If you're not sure whether you're too close,

01:40.683 --> 01:43.609
you're too close. Pull ahead to open ground before

01:43.609 --> 01:46.021
you raise a bed or climb up on a load.

01:47.146 --> 01:49.908
Our sites have overhead lines in these areas.

01:49.908 --> 01:52.658
At Boonesboro, Level 1. At Dix River,

01:52.658 --> 01:55.721
the surface. At Clover Bottom, the surface and

01:55.721 --> 01:58.071
Mine number one. Your instructor will show you

01:58.071 --> 02:00.783
exactly where the lines run at your site and which

02:00.783 --> 02:03.246
roads and dump points pass under them.

02:04.384 --> 02:06.709
Sometimes work has to happen near a line,

02:06.709 --> 02:09.297
like moving the drill or setting up a crane.

02:09.297 --> 02:11.822
That's planned in advance with your supervisor.

02:11.822 --> 02:14.472
The power company may need to deenergize or move

02:14.472 --> 02:17.584
the line. A spotter on the ground, whose only job

02:17.584 --> 02:20.434
is watching the gap, guides the operator.

02:20.434 --> 02:23.947
Masts, booms and beds are lowered before travel.

02:25.078 --> 02:27.740
Try this one. A driver's load is sticking,

02:27.740 --> 02:29.553
and he's stopped right under a line.

02:29.553 --> 02:31.765
He wants to raise the bed and bump it to shake

02:31.765 --> 02:34.215
the load loose. What's the right call?

02:34.215 --> 02:37.178
Don't raise it there. Pull ahead to open ground,

02:37.178 --> 02:39.540
well away from the line, and deal with the load

02:39.540 --> 02:42.390
there. If the bed is already partly up,

02:42.390 --> 02:45.040
lower it fully before you move, and never drive

02:45.040 --> 02:47.678
with a bed raised. Bumping a raised bed makes it

02:47.678 --> 02:50.565
bounce higher than you'd expect, and the line doesn't

02:50.565 --> 02:52.240
have to be touched to arc.

02:53.405 --> 02:56.268
Here's a slower hazard. A loader operator is stacking

02:56.268 --> 02:59.468
a stockpile near a line. Every load makes the pile

02:59.468 --> 03:02.093
taller, so he has to raise the bucket higher,

03:02.093 --> 03:04.968
and the gap to the line gets smaller every hour.

03:04.968 --> 03:07.830
Nobody made a single bad decision, but by the end

03:07.830 --> 03:10.405
of the shift the bucket is in the danger zone.

03:10.405 --> 03:13.693
If you see a pile, a berm or a road surface building

03:13.693 --> 03:17.068
up under a line, stop and tell your supervisor.

03:17.068 --> 03:19.430
Clearance doesn't stay the same just because the

03:19.430 --> 03:20.893
line didn't move.

03:24.218 --> 03:26.568
If your equipment contacts a line, stay in the

03:26.568 --> 03:30.230
cab. The ground around the machine may be energized,

03:30.230 --> 03:32.468
and the moment you step down with one foot on the

03:32.468 --> 03:35.268
machine and one on the ground, your body becomes

03:35.268 --> 03:38.480
the path. Inside the cab you're usually safe.

03:38.480 --> 03:41.080
Try to move the equipment away from the line only

03:41.080 --> 03:44.393
if it's safe to do so. Warn everyone to stay away,

03:44.393 --> 03:47.555
and radio for help so the power company can deenergize

03:47.555 --> 03:48.380
the line.

03:49.508 --> 03:52.008
Leave only if the equipment is on fire.

03:52.008 --> 03:54.670
Then jump clear, so you never touch the machine

03:54.670 --> 03:57.233
and the ground at the same time. Land with both

03:57.233 --> 04:00.545
feet together. Then shuffle away with small steps,

04:00.545 --> 04:03.383
keeping both feet on the ground and together,

04:03.383 --> 04:05.595
until you're well clear. Don't run.

04:05.595 --> 04:08.570
Long steps can put your feet at different voltages.

04:09.686 --> 04:12.123
If you see contact, don't run to help.

04:12.123 --> 04:14.861
Stay back, keep others back, and wait until the

04:14.861 --> 04:18.099
power company confirms the line is deenergized.

04:18.099 --> 04:20.636
A rescuer who touches the machine becomes the next

04:20.636 --> 04:21.423
victim.

04:22.544 --> 04:25.656
After a storm, or after equipment hits a pole,

04:25.656 --> 04:28.331
you may find a line down on the ground or sagging

04:28.331 --> 04:31.481
low over a road. Treat every downed or sagging

04:31.481 --> 04:35.006
line as live. Stay well back, keep traffic away,

04:35.006 --> 04:37.581
and report it right away by radio. Don't drive

04:37.581 --> 04:40.056
over it or under it, and don't try to move it with

04:40.056 --> 04:42.819
a stick, a bucket or anything else.

04:42.819 --> 04:45.531
Only the power company decides when it's safe.

04:46.658 --> 04:49.520
Look up before you raise anything. No dumping,

04:49.520 --> 04:53.408
parking, raised beds or tarping near overhead lines.

04:53.408 --> 04:56.383
If contact happens, stay in the cab and warn people

04:56.383 --> 04:59.233
away. If there's a fire, jump clear with your feet

04:59.233 --> 05:01.070
together and shuffle away.

05:02.204 --> 05:05.366
Remember: your site's plans and procedures always

05:05.366 --> 05:06.866
override this video.
